        <dc:title>How Sovereign Is Sovereign Credit Risk?</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>We study the nature of sovereign credit risk using an extensive set of sovereign CDS data. We find that the majority of sovereign credit risk can be linked to global factors. A single principal component accounts for 64 percent of the variation in sovereign credit spreads. Furthermore, sovereign credit spreads are more related to the US stock and high-yield markets than they are to local economic measures.
We decompose credit spreads into their risk premium and default risk components. On average, the risk premium represents about a third of the credit spread. (JEL F34, G15, O16, O19, P34)</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2011/04</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/mac.3.2.75</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
